.TH CCOL_MEMPOOL_ALLOC_ENTRY 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
ccol_mempool_alloc_entry \- allocate an entry from the pool
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cmempool.h>

void *ccol_mempool_alloc_entry(ccol_mempool *mp);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Returns a pointer to a free entry from the pool. The function allocates
from the heap when the pool is empty and the fallback is on. The contents
of the entry are uninitialized.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
mp   Memory pool to allocate from
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
Returns a pointer to the allocated entry on success
Returns NULL when the pool is empty and the fallback is off
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
O(1) complexity
The memory that comes back is uninitialized
The pointer that comes back is aligned for any object type. That is the
same guarantee that malloc() makes. Any object that you can store in
heap memory can go into a pool entry
The caller must free the entry with ccol_mempool_free_entry()

On a thread-safe pool this draws from a small cache of the calling
thread. It reaches the shared free list only when that cache needs a
refill. A pool too small to keep a cache, which means fewer than 8
elements, takes the lock of the pool on every call instead. So does a
thread that the pool has no cache left to give: one lock for each call,
as on a pool without a cache. A refill, and every call that must go past
the cache, costs one lock. See ccol_mempool_create(3) for how to size a
pool. That page also asks you
to measure a candidate configuration rather than assume one.
.fi
.SH "THREAD SAFETY"
.nf
Thread-safe if the pool was created with single_threaded = false.
Concurrent callers do not contend on the common path.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ccol_mempool_calloc_entry (3),
.BR ccol_mempool_free_entry (3),
.BR ccol_mempool_create (3)
